  "notes": "pc-r007: Transition supervision vs absolute supervision (2 arms x 3 seeds, bf16, A100). Architecture/data/optimizer IDENTICAL to pc-r006 (L=16, fixed K=4, 100k train, dim=256, concat encoder, n_recursions=8). Single contrast isolated: supervising STATES (absolute step-indexed s-hop targets, arm='absolute', pc-r006 scheme) vs supervising the OPERATOR (step-0 absolute anchor + step-invariant one-hop-advance transition loss, steps 1-7, arm='transition'). Everything else held fixed across arms and across seeds 42/43/44. bf16 autocast (no GradScaler) replaces pc-r006's fp16+scaler for numerical stability. z_aux_acc added (the metric pc-r006 finding 5 mistakenly thought it had); z_decode_acc renamed z_taskdecode_acc per the pc-r006 errata. Probe now trains at every step against saturated s-hop targets so late-step readouts are honest. Held-out-K eval (K=2,3 downward transfer) added, training K fixed at 4. Fixed data_seed=42 for all train/val/K=2/K=3 dataset construction across all 6 runs (2 arms x 3 seeds); `seed` (42/43/44) governs ONLY model init and minibatch order, so seed variance means exactly one thing (optimization trajectory), not resampled data. Questions: (1) Does z_probe_acc extend past the 2-hop boundary under transition supervision where absolute supervision (pc-r006) stalled? (2) Does val accuracy lift with the train/val gap closing (not just train climbing)? (3) Does halt_prob behavior differ by arm?",
